%Currently 150 of an allowed 150 words for Ecology Letters
\begin{abstract}
\noindent Non-native annual grasses often exhibit priority effects - historically contingent competitive
exclusion. While priority effects across species result in local exclusion, at larger spatial scales a
pattern of stable spatial monoculture patchiness may emerge. However, observations in
California rangelands reveal a possible bistable switching between invasive medusahead and
naturalized forage grass, wild oats. Here, we show through simulations of a simple two-species
Lotka-Volterra model that positive endogenous feedbacks emerge with increasing spatial scale
to maintain spatiotemporal coexistence. Many spatial coexistence studies assume external
forces as a driver of spatial heterogeneity. Our study highlights that endogenous processes
alone can maintain coexistence across space despite the common requirement of exogenous
drivers.
\end{abstract}


