\textcolor{blue}{what I have previously written..based around priority effects and alternative stable states}

Invasive species often demonstrate strong priority effects, wherein their competitive advantages
during arrival and establishment induce historically contingent competitive exclusion, a process
where species arrivals determine community assembly outcomes (Hess et al. 2019). Strong and
persistent priority effects can create alternative stable states. States switches then demand
large perturbations and native species re-establishment likelihood is reduced, even when
environmental conditions are favorable (Gulpin and Case 1976; Tilman 1988). Spatially,
alternative stable states may produce a stable patchiness pattern across a landscape where the
monoculture state is initial abundance dependent (Shurin et al. 2004). That is, while priority
effects beget local exclusion, regional spatial monoculture patchiness may emerge, a process
that is spatial heterogeneity dependent.

Various theories have been developed and extended to account for fluctuations across
space, for example, developments in theory of the spatial storage effect and metacommunity
coexistence (Chesson, 2000a; Shoemaker and Melbourne, 2016). Space inclusion often involves
a reliance on some presumed external drivers (e.g., climatic, trophic) which govern spatial
(Leibold et al., 2004). Spatial coexistence theories emphasize the role of environmental
variability in maintaining species diversity, for instance differences in resource availability,
habitat conditions, or disturbance regimes across space may create opportunities for species to
persististence (Chesson, 2000). The reliance on spatial heterogeneity assumes that
environmental variation is essential for coexistence by providing niches or refuges that prevent
competitive exclusion. While the importance of environmental variability is a reasonable
assumption, other bodies of work highlight emergent dynamics with increased spatial scale,
even in the absence of environmental variability (Levin, 1992).

Emergent dynamics arise from intrinsic interactions among species and individuals.
Individuals can modify their environment through positive feedbacks, and through these
endogenous feedbacks alone, mechanisms can emerge that maintain species persistence in
the absence of external variation. Research in positive endogenous feedbacks, highlighting that
variation can occur through entirely internal biotic processes, is exemplified by plant-soil
feedbacks of exotic invaders, where individuals modify the soil microbial community that in
effect is beneficial to themselves (Levine et al., 2006; van der Putten et al., 2013). The
importance of plant-soil feedbacks for invasion success has been put to the test via two
hypotheses: individuals that have experienced the feedback, and are the most demographically
successful, disperse and “push” forward exotic species invasion. Alternatively, if feedbacks are
not the mechanism for invasive spread, individuals in the frontline that would have not
experienced the feedback, nevertheless contribute most to dispersal and ”pull” the invasion
forward (Levine et al., 2006). These hypotheses assume a single colonizing species and that
feedbacks are species-specific (i.e., only beneficial to that species), contributing to invasive
monoculture dominance; however, when feedbacks are positive across species, priority effects
may be disrupted and fluctuating bi-stability may emerge.

Here, we compare a simple two-species Lotka-Volterra competition model and an
extended version that is entirely driven by spatially structured endogenous feedbacks to assess
the conditions under which push and pull mechanisms of positive endogenous feedbacks
overcome priority effects and contribute to species coexistence. Our simulations aim to model
an example involving positive litter feedbacks where two competing annual grasses coexist in
patches across California rangelands: Medusahead (MH, Taentheranium caput-medusae),
noxious invasive, and Avena (AV, Avena barbata), naturalized forage. MH exhibits signs of
positive feedbacks with itself that make it a successful invasive, largely attributed to its high
silica content, making it unpalatable to grazers and slow to decompose. Slow decomposition
causes large buildups of MH litter and hinders the growth of other species, while benefiting both
itself and interestingly, AV (Mariotte et al., 2017).

While AV has historically been the competitive dominant of California rangelands,
outcompeting native grasses and forbs since its introduction, more recent MH invasion has
triggered patch transitions, a system switch previously described using alternative stable states
with almost irreversible transitions back to AV (George et al., 1992). However, more recent
observations describe dramatic, symmetric and rapid patch switching between AV and MH
(Stein et al., 2016). Experimentally tested positive feedbacks between litter depth and both MH
and AV vital rates (Mariotte et al., 2017) may explain a possible “push” and “pull” mechanism
generating the observed patch switching over time (\ref{fig:grassdiagram}). 
AV germination fraction increases in higher litter depths, 
thus AV may be pulled from its frontline where there is no litter into MH
patches with litter. On the other hand, MH germination is not related to litter depth but MH seed
production is, pushing MH out of its former patches. The cycle repeats as AV and MH disperse
and invade into each other and swap places. Given the litter feedback dynamics between AV
and MH and dispersal’s important role, we hypothesize that 1) positive litter feedbacks emerge
with increasing spatial scale and thus 2) spatially structured positive litter feedbacks maintain
global coexistence between AV and MH despite local competitive exclusion.

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Population growth}
To investigate the role of endogenous feedbacks on spatial coexistence, 
we expanded a simple Lotka-Volterra model (ref) to describe an example case study of two annual grass species \emph{Avena} and Medusahead, $N_i \in {1,2}$, that respond to grass litter accumulation via different vital rates. 
For simplicity, the population of $N_i$ at time $t+1$ depends on its intrinsic growth $\lambda_i$, and interspecific competition $\beta_{ij}$. 

\begin{equation}
N_{i,t+1} = N_{i,t} \cdot \lambda_i \cdot \left(1 - N_{i,t} - \beta_{ij} \cdot N_{j,t} \right)
\end{equation}

The model was non-dimenionalized such that $\beta_{ij} = \frac{\alpha_{ij}}{\alpha_{jj}}$ of the standard LV competition model. Germination was not included for simplicity, although species 2 responds to litter through
germination. Instead, species 2 response to litter is modified through dispersal of germinants. 
%explain something about germinants model
\subsection{Space and dispersal}
We allow for environmentally biased dispersal.

Let $N_i(x,t)$ be the abundance of species $i$ in cell $x$ at time $t$,  
$D_i$ the proportion of the population dispersing per time step,  
$E(x)$ the environmental value at cell $x$,  
$\theta$ the bias strength parameter,  
and $\mathcal{N}(x)$ the set of neighboring cells of $x$.

Let:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $N_i(x,t)$ = abundance of species $i$ in cell $x$ at time $t$
    \item $D_i$ = fraction of the population dispersing per time step
    \item $E(x)$ = environmental value at cell $x$
    \item $\theta$ = bias strength parameter
    \item $\mathcal{N}(x)$ = set of neighboring cells of $x$
    \item $\mathcal{N}(o)$ = set of neighboring cells of origin $o$ (can include $o$ itself if self-retention is allowed)
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{1. Destination weights}
For each destination cell $d \in \mathcal{N}(o)$ of origin cell $o$, assign an unnormalized weight:
\[
w(d) = \exp\big( \theta \, E(d) \big).
\]

\paragraph{2. Movement probability}
The probability of moving from origin $o$ to destination $d$ is:
\[
p_{o \to d} = \frac{w(d)}{\sum\limits_{y \in \mathcal{N}(o)} w(y)}
= \frac{\exp\big( \theta E(d) \big)}
{\sum\limits_{y \in \mathcal{N}(o)} \exp\big( \theta E(y) \big)}.
\]

Note that:
\[
\sum_{d \in \mathcal{N}(o)} p_{o \to d} = 1
\]
for each origin $o$.

\paragraph{3. Biased diffusion update}
The biased diffusion step for species $i$ is:
\[
N_i(x, t+1) = (1 - D_i) \, N_i(x, t) 
+ D_i \sum_{o \in \mathcal{N}(x)} N_i(o, t) \, p_{o \to x}.
\]

Substituting $p_{o \to x}$:
\[
N_i(x, t+1) = (1 - D_i) \, N_i(x, t) 
+ D_i \sum_{o \in \mathcal{N}(x)} 
\frac{N_i(o, t) \, \exp\big( \theta E(x) \big)}
{\sum\limits_{y \in \mathcal{N}(o)} \exp\big( \theta E(y) \big)}.
\]

\textbf{English explanation:}

For each patch $x$, there are neighbors $o$, that are the \emph{origin} of inflow to $x$. 
The inflow to $x$ is, for each possible neighboring origin
$o$, the population density of $o$ weighted by the 
environmental attraction of $x$ [$\exp(\theta E(x))$] that is 
scaled by the environmental attraction of the neighbors of 
$o$. Note that when $\theta=0$, $p$ is uniform across neighbors and dispersal is unbiased.


\subsection{Litter}
Across space, Avena and Medusahead are boosted in high litter patches, although, differently, though different stage-structured vital rates. 
In our case study, litter is created endogenously and feed back to influence population dynamics. Litter at time $t+1$, is simply a scalar multiple, $l_i$ of $N_i$ at time $t$. The scalar, $l_i$ represents a fatal conversion of living grass individuals to unit of litter. 

\begin{equation}
L_{t+1} = \sum l_i \cdot N_{i,t}
\end{equation}

Yes, this is really simply and doesn't allow accumulation and decay. I have toyed with accumulation at time $t+2$ as litter at time $t+1$ multiplied by $d$, a decay constant. So $L_{t+2} = \sum l_i \cdot N_{i,t+1} + d \cdot L_{t+1}$.
\subsubsection{Litter differences}
We chose to define litter feedbacks as a function of neighbor litter differences, $\Delta_L,x$. 
\begin{equation}
\Delta_L(x,t) = \sum_{x' \in \mathcal{N}(x)}L(x',t)-L(x,t)
\end{equation}
So a patch, $x$ with high $\Delta_L$ means that it has more litter than its (8) neighbors. 

\subsubsection{Litter boost $\lambda$ in species 1}
When litter is high, Medusahead is boosted in seed production. 
We used a logistic function to model how its baseline $\lambda_1^0$ is modifed by litter. Precisley, the modification depends on $\Delta_L,x$, the summed difference between the current patch and neighboring patches, such that a patch with more litter on average that its neigbors, $\lambda_1$ will be boosted greater than $\lambda_1^0$. But a patch with less litter on average than its neighbors will reduce $\lambda_1$ to less than $\lambda_1^0$. 
The logistic function is ideal in that it is naturally asymptotic and bounded between zero and the numerator. The rate at which it reaches the asymptotes is defined by $\rho$, which in our example represents the degree in which litter influences $\lambda_1$.
\begin{equation}
\lambda_1(\Delta_L) = \frac{(\lambda_1^0-1)2}{1+e^{-\rho \Delta_L}}
\end{equation}
The numerator, $(\lambda_1^0-1)2$, maintains $\lambda_1^0$ at $\Delta_L=0$.
\subsubsection{Litter attracts germinants of sp2}
For species 1 and all no litter models, $\theta=0$ in the dispersal function, indicating non-environmentally
biased dispersal. But for species 2, $\theta>0$ and its germinants are attracted to litter, 
as shown for Avena (Mariotte 2016). We define $E_x$ as the sum of relativised neighbor litter ranking. 

\subsection{Simulation}
For each simulation, the model was integrated using a forward over a duration sufficient for
system stabilization (typically 15–30 time units). As in the model, dispersal was implemented after
deterministic growth and competition within each patch. Grid edges were either treated as
true zeros (such that a corner patch has only three neighbors: two adjacent and one diagonal) 
or torodial (edge neighbors wrap around to the other side). 
Initial conditions were randomized as follows. Across patches, one species was introduced at high
density (e.g., 0.70), while the other was absent. The species populations were allowed to grow 
without competition and without dispersal for an amount of time, $t_D$, i.e., 
monoclture burn-in time. At time, $t=t_D$ a small amount of the other species was
introduced and dispersal was allowed. We repeated simulations across a range of
spatial extents (from 1 to 100 patches) and with and without space (dispersal) or litter feedbacks, 
for a neutral, competitive, and priority effects model. List parameters space: 
$\lambda^1_0 \in [1,3], \beta_{ij} \in [0.5,1.5], \theta \in [0,6]$, etc...

\subsection{Analysis}
Across model parameters and for each spatial scale and litter regime, we recorded: 
1) local and regional species abundances, 
2) species occupancy (number of patches where each species persisted), and 
3) coexistence probability (proportion of patches where both species persisted at
above 20 percent of total patch abundance). 
\textcolor{blue}{This is a place holder and I need to think more about what analysis/calculations I want 
to do on the simulated data to easily show trends acorss parameter ranges.}
These metrics were computed at equilibrium across 50 replicate simulations per treatment. 
This design allowed us to quantify the scale-dependence of litter feedbacks as a mechanism of coexistence.

\section{Results}
