\subsection{Non-spatial models}
Populations stabilize at analytical equilibriums.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Extinction}: $(N_1^*, N_2^*) = (0, 0)$.
  \item \textbf{Species 1 only}: if $\lambda_1 > 1$, then
  \[
  (N_1^*, N_2^*) = \left(1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_1}, 0\right).
  \]
  \item \textbf{Species 2 only}: if $\lambda_2 > 1$, then
  \[
  (N_1^*, N_2^*) = \left(0, 1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_2} \right).
  \]
  \item \textbf{Coexistence}: 
  \[
  (N_1^*, N_2^*) = \big(\frac{1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_1}- \beta_{12}(1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_2})}
  {1 + \beta_{12} \beta_{21}}, 
  \frac{1- \frac{1}{\lambda_2}- \beta_{21}(1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_1})}
  {1 + \beta_{12} \beta_{21}} \big).
  \]
\end{itemize}

When $\beta_{12}=\beta_{21}>1$, leads to priority effects. 
Winner reaches monoculture equilibrium and looser goes to zero.
See in app. 
The identity of winner depends on initial density. 
However, global density is at the coexistence equilibrium.

