\subsection{Litter feedbacks enable dynamic patch switching and global coexistence}
In contrast, incorporating litter dynamics significantly altered competitive outcomes (Figure X).
When litter accumulation positively influenced the intrinsic growth rate of 
species 1 (via parameter $\rho$) and the germinant diffusion of species 2 
(via $\theta$), the model generated conditions under which 
local dominance could shift over time due to legacy 
effects of past occupancy. These feedbacks created temporally 
dynamic advantages that allowed both species to re-
invade patches they had previously been excluded from (Figure X).
Under neutral parameterizations (equal intrinsic growth rates, and
competition coefficients $\beta_{ij}=1$),  
litter feedbacks produced stable coexistence across the landscape,
with patch occupancy by each species fluctuating over time but 
%neither species going extinct.
sp2 is never greater than sp1 and is slowing going extinct Fig.\ref{fig:neutral}.
Species 2 $\lambda$ must be boosted to reach densities similar to sp1 and maintain 
stability (visual, at least in 100 times). Fig. \ref{fig:neutral-ish}.
Even under strong priority effects ($\beta_{12}$ and $\beta_{21} >1$), coexistence was maintained in
(some percent) of simulations when dispersal was moderate and litter feedbacks were sufficiently strong
($\rho \geq X(1?), \theta \geq X, D>X)$. 
In these scenarios, dominance periodically shifted between species within
patches, (and adjacent patches are opposite), 
and regional coexistence was stabilized through asynchronous patch dynamics (Figure ).

\subsection{Parameter regimes supporting coexistence}
Coexistence occurred within specific ranges of the feedback parameters and dispersal
rates. 
When litter feedbacks were weak ($\rho < X$, $\theta < X$), or when dispersal was either too
limited or too widespread, coexistence broke down and one species was excluded. 
Too little dispersal prevented recolonization of newly favorable patches, 
while excessive dispersal homogenized the system and re-established competitive exclusion. 
Optimal coexistence was observed at intermediate dispersal (X–X fraction of individuals moving per timestep) 
and moderate litter feedback strength ( $\rho \in {0.2, 0.4}; \theta \in {0.3, 0.6}$).

\subsection{When dynamics are synchronous}
Patch switching dynamics produce temporal and spatial asynchrony. When one species grows in a patch, the other declines, and species identities are opposed in adjacent patches, leading to a fluctuating checkerboard pattern over term. 
Changes to population parameters or simulation design can lead to synchronous dynamics instead. 
\begin{itemize}
\item When burn in time is low, <3 Fig \ref{fig:noburnin}. Fig \ref{fig:burnin1wrap0}
\item When litter can accumulate and decays at time 2 <.87 ish. Fig \ref{fig:d20.2wraptord}, \ref{fig:d20.2wrap0}
