\section{Discussion}
\textcolor{blue}{old discussion framed around the species having strong priority effects..}

Our results demonstrate that spatial processes and feedbacks from litter accumulation jointly
mediate species coexistence in a competitive system \sout{where priority effects} that would otherwise
lead to exclusion. Specifically, we found that while \sout{priority effects} exclusions dominate in non-spatial
models and spatial models without endogenously driven feedbacks, the addition of biologically
plausible litter-mediated feedbacks enables dynamic patch turnover and long-term coexistence
across the landscape (Fig XX). Our findings suggest that coexistence emerges from the
interaction between spatial processes, temporal endogenous environmental modification (via
litter), and competition.

While models without litter feedbacks, with and without spatial structure, resulted in no
coexistence, in contrast, adding spatial structure with dispersal and incorporating feedbacks
from accumulated litter altered the system’s dynamics and coexistence outcomes. By allowing
for species specific stage-structured litter feedbacks where positive effects of litter on species
1’s intrinsic growth rate and species 2’s germination created shifting environmental conditions
that allowed each species to exploit the legacy of its own and its competitor’s past occupancy.
These feedbacks broke the static nature of local priority effects by enabling patch conditions to
change over time, thus allowing competitors to recolonize and persist. Here, litter functions as a
time-varying environmental modifier, introducing a form of endogenous niche construction
(Odling-Smee et al. 2003) that stabilizes coexistence through temporal niche differentiation.

Without space or litter feedbacks, outcomes aligned with classical theory: strong priority
effects led to exclusion, even under neutral parameters where stochasticity alone broke
symmetry (REF). These results reinforce that in closed, homogeneous systems, initial
conditions and competitive hierarchies dictate long-term outcomes (Ke and Letten 2018). Adding
spatial structure and dispersal allowed for local variation but did not enable regional
coexistence. Consistent with previous work, space alone was insufficient to overcome
deterministic priority effects when interspecific competition exceeded intraspecific competition
(Amarasekare 2003; Levine et al. 2004).

This study extends prior work by explicitly modeling how litter-mediated feedbacks can
modulate demographic rates in spatially structured environments. Our study specifically
illuminates possible dynamics between noxious medusahead and naturalized forage Avena in
California rangelands where frequent switching between patches of the two annual grasses has
been observed (Stein et al. 2016) and litter feedbacks and stage-structured vital rates has been
experimentally tested (Mariotte et al. 2017). In empirical systems such as California grasslands,
where litter can suppress or promote germination and growth (Dyer and Rice 1997; Eviner and
Hawkes 2008), our findings suggest a plausible mechanism for the species persistence despite
competitive pressure from successful invaders. Importantly, the species-specific effects of litter
–benefiting different life stages in each species– underscore the importance of considering
feedback heterogeneity when evaluating coexistence mechanisms.

Similar to the spatial storage effect (Chesson 2000), a fluctuation dependent mechanism
of coexistence, asynchronous dynamics across patches can buffer species against regional
extinction. However, unique to our study is that these dynamics arise from endogenously
generated feedbacks rather than fixed environmental heterogeneity. In classic storage effect
models, coexistence relies on externally imposed spatial or temporal variation. Here, litter-
mediated feedbacks create shifting local conditions that favor different species over time,
enabling patch turnover without any imposed environmental variation. This self-generated
heterogeneity allows inferior competitors to persist by exploiting the biotic legacies of prior
dominance, highlighting the potential for niche construction to stabilize coexistence in spatial
systems.

While our model captures key dynamics relevant to priority effects and spatial
coexistence, it abstracts away several ecological complexities. For example, we assume
constant parameter values for feedback strengths, while in nature, these ostensibly vary with
fluctuation in precipitation (Stein et al. 2016), increasing mean annual temperature, or microbial
mediation (Gornish et al. 2016). Additionally, we consider only two species, though real
communities involve complex multi-species interactions that could amplify or dampen the
feedbacks we describe. While here we focused on the two dominating species in California
rangelands and specifically litter feedbacks, observed communities may include more species,
potentially increasing competitive pressures (Chesson 1994) and neutralizing the coexistence
contribution of litter feedbacks. Microbial interactions can also neutralize litter feedbacks, for
example, MH might experience negative plant-soil feedbacks, associating with undesirable
microbes over time, causing a pull out of its patches (Forero et al. In prep.).
In conclusion, our findings highlight how the interaction of spatial dynamics with
endogenously mediated feedbacks can reverse exclusionary outcomes driven by priority effects.
Coexistence, in this context, does not emerge from static niche differentiation but from temporal
patch turnover driven by biotic legacies. This suggests that restoration and management efforts
aiming to support coexistence may benefit from enhancing or mimicking such feedbacks, for
instance, by manipulating litter levels or promoting species-specific germination cues across a
heterogeneous landscape.

